  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 144 AM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\nFor the past several months, my strings of shifts seemed like they \nhave been one extreme or another, not much in between. In May, they \nwere quiet. In June, fairly busy with predicting strong to severe \nthunderstorms. From July through most of September, it was the \nseemingly never ending monsoon with periods of showers and \nthunderstorms that the models handled rather poorly. And this was \nalso sprinkled with record heat, including some all time record high \ntemperatures.\n\nMost of this string has returned to the quiet type, with only one \nnight of somewhat active weather. And this will continue through the \nweekend. For today, a weak cold front will slide southward today, \ncoming to rest around the Continental Divide. This will mean cooler \ntemperatures East of the Divide, anywhere from 5 to 8 degrees in \ncentral Wyoming to as much as 15 degrees in Johnson County. \nTemperatures will still average above normal for this time of year \nthough. There will be locally breezy conditions in the favored \nnorthwest flow areas, especially Johnson County and eastern Fremont \n/ western Natrona Counties, where there is a greater than 3 out of 4 \nchance of gusts over 35 mph. The strongest gusts should be in \nJohnson County, where gusts over 40 mph are possible. No real fire \nweather concerns though, since humidity will be higher behind the \ncold front and wind should be light to moderate across southern \nWyoming.\n\nSunday is shaping up to be one of those nearly perfect early autumn \ndays with a cool start in the morning, a comfortably warm afternoon, \nlots of sunshine and  a fairly light wind. So, if you are fortunate \nenough to have the day off, enjoy it. We don't get many like it in \nWyoming.\n\nWe do have some concerns for Monday though. A trough will be moving \nout of the west coast and approach the northern and central Rockies \nMonday and Tuesday. The system has little moisture to work with and \nany moisture it has will likely remain south of Wyoming. It will \ntighten the pressure gradient across the area though, especially in \nthe favored southwest flow locations, like from Muddy Gap through \nCasper. This should bring a gusty breeze, with the NBM ensemble \nshowing a greater than 3 out of 4 chance of gusts over 30 mph from \nRock Springs through Casper as well as the Lee of the Absarokas on \nMonday afternoon. Downsloping flow will also bring a very warm day, \nespecially for early October. Most locations East of the Divide \nbelow 6000 feet have at least a 1 in 2 chance of highs over 80, with \nthe warmer spots like Thermopolis, Worland and even Buffalo having a \ngreater than 1 in 2 chance of highs of 85 degrees or greater. This \nwill also bring low humidity, under 15 percent in some locations. We \nmay need some fire weather watches for this day but it is a bit too \nearly at this time. The most likely area of concern would be from \nEastern Sweetwater County through Natrona County.\n\nTuesday looks warm but a little cooler. Wind also looks to be a \nlittle less and humidity a bit higher, making things borderline for \nany fire weather highlights. Much of next week looks the same, with \na continuation of above normal temperatures and occasionally breezy \nconditions that could bring elevated fire weather each\nafternoon.",
